447	Instructions for Payee
448	You have received this form because you have either (a) accepted
449	payment cards for payments, or (b) received payments through a third
450	party network in the calendar year reported on this form. Merchant
451	acquirers and third party settlement organizations, as payment
452	settlement entities (PSEs), must report the proceeds of payment card
453	and third party network transactions made to you on Form 1099-K
454	under Internal Revenue Code section 6050W. The PSE may have
455	contracted with an electronic payment facilitator (EPF) or other third
456	party payer to make payments to you.
457	If you have questions about the amounts reported on this form,
458	contact the FILER whose information is shown in the upper left corner
459	on the front of this form. If you do not recognize the FILER shown in
460	the upper left corner of the form, contact the PSE whose name and
461	phone number are shown in the lower left corner of the form above
462	your account number.
463	Note: For more information on why you received your Form 1099-K,
464	go to www.irs.gov/KnowYour1099K. For information on what to do
465	with your Form 1099-K, go to www.irs.gov/businesses/what-to-dowith-form-1099-k.
466	If the Form 1099-K is related to your business, see Pub. 334 for
467	more information. If the Form 1099-K is related to your work as part of
468	the gig economy, go to www.irs.gov/GigEconomy.
469	See the separate instructions for your income tax return for using
470	the information reported on this form.
471	Payee’s taxpayer identification number (TIN). For your protection,
472	this form may show only the last four digits of your TIN (social security
473	number (SSN), individual taxpayer identification number (ITIN),
474	adoption taxpayer identification number (ATIN), or employer
475	identification number (EIN)). However, the issuer has reported your
476	complete TIN to the IRS.
477	Account number. May show an account number or other unique
478	number the PSE assigned to distinguish your account.
479	Box 1a. Shows the aggregate gross amount of payment card/third
480	party network transactions made to you through the PSE during the
481	calendar year.
482	
483	Note: The gross amount is the total dollar amount of total reportable
484	payment transactions without regard to any adjustments for credits,
485	cash equivalents, discount amounts, fees, refunded amounts,
486	shipping amounts, or any other amounts. The dollar amount of each
487	transaction is determined on the date of the transaction.
488	Box 1b. Shows the aggregate gross amount of all reportable payment
489	transactions made to you through the PSE during the calendar year
490	where the card was not present at the time of the transaction or the
491	card number was keyed into the terminal. Typically, this relates to
492	online sales, phone sales, or catalogue sales. If the box for third party
493	network is checked, or if these are third party network transactions,
494	Card Not Present transactions will not be reported.
495	Box 2. Shows the merchant category code used for payment card/
496	third party network transactions (if available) reported on this form.
497	Box 3. Shows the number of payment transactions (not including
498	refund transactions) processed through the payment card/third party
499	network.
500	Box 4. Shows backup withholding. Generally, a payer must backup
501	withhold if you did not furnish your TIN or you did not furnish the
502	correct TIN to the payer. See Form W-9, Request for Taxpayer
503	Identification Number and Certification, and Pub. 505. Include this
504	amount on your income tax return as tax withheld.
505	Boxes 5a–5l. Show the gross amount of payment card/third party
506	network transactions made to you for each month of the calendar
507	year.
508	Boxes 6–8. Show state and local income tax withheld from the
509	payments.
510	Future developments. For the latest information about developments
511	related to Form 1099-K and its instructions, such as legislation
512	enacted after they were published, go to www.irs.gov/Form1099K.
513	Free File Program. Go to www.irs.gov/FreeFile to see if you qualify
514	for no-cost online federal tax preparation, e-filing, and direct deposit
515	or payment options.
